## 5. Judgement calls

| Decision | Choice | Why |
|---|---|---|
| **Part 2 question** | 2(a) in full; 2(b) screened only | 2(a) links directly to the year's goal and to the brief's warning about install mix. 2(b) is screened at the end of `02_deep_dive.ipynb` and on slide 7. |
| **Unit for ARPDAU** | Country tier, not country | As the brief advises. |
| **ARPDAU uncertainty** | Player-level Poisson bootstrap (1,000 draws) | Daily ARPDAU follows the 15-day sale cycle, so days are not independent observations. Players are resampled instead. |
| **Channel comparison** | Direct standardisation to the pooled tier mix; stratified player bootstrap (5,000 draws) | Adding install month as a second stratum gives almost the same result. |
| **Multiple comparisons** | Holm correction on D7 across the 3 channel pairs | Organic vs paid_video D7: p = 0.028 raw, 0.083 after Holm. |
| **Lapse window** | 14 days | 89% of gaps between play days are ≤ 14 days, so 14 silent days is a real break. 79% of players return even after 28 silent days, so a longer window gives no cleaner "gone" state. |
| **Snapshots** | Weekly, not daily | Daily snapshots give heavy players many near-duplicate rows. |
| **Model validation** | Time split with a one-week embargo | No training label overlaps the test period. |
| **Model choice** | Interpretable logistic regression | The alternative scores only +0.01 AUC but includes level and tenure (correlation 0.88), which makes its coefficients flip sign. |
| **Sale-cycle baseline** | Mean ARPDAU on cycle days 5–10 | The mid-cycle days are furthest from any sale. The offset (~78%) depends on this choice, which is one reason 2(b) is only screened. |
| **Part 4(b)** | Not attempted | Only installs up to ~30 January have 90 days of data, and they predate the paid ramp. |

