## 7. Specific limitations

| # | Limitation | Effect on the conclusions |
|---|---|---|
| 7.1 | **Values are gross of app-store fees.** | Tier 4's $1.63 at 60 days is ~$1.14–1.39 net at 15–30% fees. Compare CPI with the net figure. |
| 7.2 | **Ad revenue is missing.** | Tier 4 players complete ~1.8× as many rewarded ads per active day as Tier 1, so IAP-only value understates them most. |
| 7.3 | **No value beyond day 60.** | The extract is too short for lifetime value; nothing is extrapolated. |
| 7.4 | **The 60-day value comes from Jan–Feb installs.** | These are mostly pre-ramp. Applying it to today's paid_video Tier 4 installs relies on Part 3's finding that channels do not differ at the same tier. |
| 7.5 | **The lapse value ($0.62) is an upper bound.** | It is the 30-day IAP gap between players who stayed and those who lapsed in the highest-risk fifth. Stayers are more engaged to begin with, so it is not a causal effect. |
| 7.6 | **Cap assignment is unknown.** | The cap-5 vs cap-8 comparison assumes caps are not tied to the type of player or day. |
| 7.7 | **The sale offset is a screen, not an estimate.** | It depends on the assumed "normal" level and ranges 51–104% across the 7 cycles. A 15-day vs 30-day cadence test is needed. |
